      {
        "id": "v2-2c897-p51-practice-slot-14-slot-14-q-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$e^{2\\ln x}=3x+10$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=5$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}e^{2\\ln x}&=3x+10\\\\e^{\\ln x^2}&=3x+10\\\\x^2&=3x+10\\\\x^2-3x-10&=0\\\\(x-5)(x+2)&=0\\end{align*}$$So $x=5$ or $x=-2$. Since $x>0$, $x=5$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 45
      },
      {
        "id": "v2-2c897-p51-practice-slot-14-slot-14-q-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$e^{\\frac12\\ln x}=x-6$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=9$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}e^{\\frac12\\ln x}&=x-6\\\\e^{\\ln\\sqrt x}&=x-6\\\\\\sqrt x&=x-6\\end{align*}$$Let $u=\\sqrt x$, where $u>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}u&=u^2-6\\\\u^2-u-6&=0\\\\(u-3)(u+2)&=0\\end{align*}$$Since $u>0$, $u=3$, so $x=9$.\n\nCheck: $\\sqrt9=3=9-6$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      }
    ]
  },
